  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Monday)\nIssued at 1254 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe main forecast impact through the extended period remains the\npotential for heavy rain and flooding, along with severe weather\npotential through the latter part of the work week. The overall\npattern is coming into a little better agreement over the last day\nor so in regards to timing of the system. A mid level low pressure\ncenter, currently centered near San Francisco will drop\nsoutheastward into the Desert Southwest over the coming day,\nAdditionally, the remnants of current Hurricane Polo off the Baja\nCalifornia Coast will drift northeastward and interact with the\napproaching potent upper low. These two systems will merge by\nTuesday into Wednesday with a steady plume of tropical moisture\nstreaming into the Southern Plains through the latter half of the\nweek. The result will be an increasing heavy rain threat\ndeveloping the second half of the week, along with a limited\nsevere potential through the period.\n\nLatest model guidance has come into better agreement on a more\nprogressive solution with the combined system. This forecast will\nbegin to trend to a bit faster solution than previous forecast,\nwhich will help limit the overall high end rainfall potential over\nparts of the area. The upper low is forecast to eject into the\nSouthern Plains on Wednesday and lift northeastward across \nOklahoma during the day Thursday. Widespread showers and storms\nare expected to initiate west of the local region on Wednesday and\ntrack toward eastern Oklahoma by late Wednesday into Thursday.\nIncreasing shear and modest instability associated with the\nejecting trough will aid in some severe potential as a line of\nstorms moves into the area Wednesday night. Damaging wind gusts\nlook to be the main threat, though low level profiles would\nsupport some QLCS tornado potential with the leading line.\nAdditional shower and storm development is likely on Thursday as\nthe trough axis swings into the region before exiting the area\nlater in the day Thursday. Saturated, tropical like profiles with\nPrecipitable Water values near the climatological max for late\nSeptember supports the potential for very heavy rainfall rates \nand potential flash flooding over the areas of heaviest rain. The\nongoing drought conditions will limit some of the flooding \npotential as it relates to mainstem rivers and streams as most are\nrunning well low at this point. Initially dry, hard ground could \nlead to excess runoff with initial storms however which could \nheighten the flash flood potential during the onset of heavier \nrain Wednesday night. \n\nShowers could linger into Friday as wrap around moisture drops\nsouth on the backside of the exiting low and a cold front moves\ninto the area. The heavy rain and severe potential will be lower \nduring this time frame, but Friday could still be a dreary day of\ncloudy, rainy, breezy conditions across the region behind the \ncold front. In terms of rainfall totals, a widespread two to five\ninches is forecast across much of the forecast area, while locally\nhigher totals of six inches plus is in the realm of possibility\nwhere more training occurs. The remains some concern that a large \ngradient in totals could set up near the I-44 corridor as a dry \nslot eats into the available moisture late Wednesday into \nThursday. We will continue to keep an eye on that scenario as it \ngets closer. The highest totals look to be across southeast \nOklahoma right now, but that placement could shift over the next \nday or so as guidance continues to get a better handle on the \nsystem.\n\nCooler and drier weather will follow the passage of the cold front\nfor the weekend into early next week. Some low chances for\nlingering showers could continue across the southern half of the\nforecast area through the weekend as the mid level trough axis\nlingers near the Red River.\n\nBowlan",
